// PENDING: from an OPEN SigmaHQ pull request. Not reviewed or merged by SigmaHQ yet.
// Pull request: #6104 Add rule: indirect command execution via scp.exe
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/6104
// Pull request author: ashish-cybersec
// Change: new rule
// Title: Indirect Command Execution Via Scp.EXE
// Sigma ID: 2a79d41a-b9c8-4a60-a467-87f934f6b4e1
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: Ashish Pulivarthy (ashish-cybersec)
// Date: 2026-07-02 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: stealth, T1202
// Description: Detects the use of the OpenSSH "scp.exe" client to execute commands indirectly, either via the
//   "-S" option (which specifies the program to use for the encrypted connection) or via a
//   "ProxyCommand". Both mechanisms allow a threat actor to abuse the trusted "scp.exe" binary as a
//   proxy to launch arbitrary programs while evading detection. This completes coverage of the
//   OpenSSH client LOLBin family alongside the existing "sftp.exe" and "ssh.exe" rules.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate use of scp with a custom transport program or proxy command. This is rare and mostly seen in constrained network or administration scenarios.
// References:
//   - https://redsiege.com/blog/2024/04/sshishing-abusing-shortcut-files-and-the-windows-ssh-client-for-initial-access/
//   - https://gtfobins.github.io/gtfobins/scp/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/d4676631046c03b58382386b81bcaf36bc603c37/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_scp_proxy_command_execution.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "\\scp.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "scp.exe") and (ProcessCommandLine contains " -S " or ProcessCommandLine contains "ProxyCommand=")
